---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def, { GRAVEL_MATERIALS } from '../calculators/gravel';
import { bulkResult, coverageFt2PerYd3 } from '../lib/bulk';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';

const ex = bulkResult(10 * 20 * 0.25, 5, 1.4, [0.5]);
const coverage = [2, 3, 4, 6].map((d) => ({ d, yd: coverageFt2PerYd3(d), ton: coverageFt2PerYd3(d) / 1.4 }));
---

<CalcPage
  slug="gravel-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Gravel Calculator: Cubic Yards, Tons & Bags for Any Area"
  description="Free gravel calculator. Enter the area and depth to get cubic yards, tons, and bags of pea gravel, crushed stone, crusher run, or river rock."
  h1="Gravel Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Work out how many cubic yards and tons of gravel you need for a driveway, path, patio, or drainage bed, and how many
    bags if the job is small.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate gravel</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Measure the length and width of the area in feet. For a curved bed, choose Circle or add several shapes to a project.</li>
    <li>Decide the depth and convert it to feet: 3 inches is 0.25 ft.</li>
    <li>Multiply length × width × depth to get cubic feet, then divide by 27 for cubic yards.</li>
    <li>Multiply cubic yards by the weight of your stone, about 1.4 tons per yard for most gravel, to get tons.</li>
    <li>Add 5–10% and round up to what your supplier sells, usually half yards or half tons.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="example">Example: a 10 × 20 ft pea gravel patio, 3 inches deep</h2>
  <p>
    10 × 20 × 0.25 = 50 cubic feet, or 1.85 cubic yards. With 5% extra that is {fmt(ex.cubicYards)} cubic yards, which
    weighs about {fmt(ex.tons)} tons at 1.4 tons per yard. Order {fmt(ex.orderYards)} yards or {fmt(ex.orderTons)} tons.
    In bags, it would take {ex.bags[0].count} half-cubic-foot bags, which is why bulk delivery wins at this size.
  </p>

  <h2 id="weight">How much does a yard of gravel weigh?</h2>
  <p>
    A cubic yard of gravel weighs about <strong>1.4 tons (2,800 lb)</strong>. The real number runs from roughly 1.3 to
    1.5 tons depending on the stone size, how much fine material is mixed in, and how wet it is. Crusher run and road base
    are heavier because the fines fill the gaps between stones.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Material</th><th class="num">Typical tons per cubic yard</th><th class="num">Cubic yards per ton</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {GRAVEL_MATERIALS.map((m) => <tr><td>{m.label}</td><td class="num">{m.tonsPerYd3}</td><td class="num">{fmt(1 / m.tonsPerYd3)}</td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Quarries and landscape yards publish their own weights. If yours does, pick <strong>Custom weight</strong> in the calculator and enter it.</p>

  <h2 id="coverage">How much area does a ton of gravel cover?</h2>
  <p>
    At 1.4 tons per yard, one ton of gravel covers about <strong>77 square feet at 3 inches deep</strong> and about 116
    square feet at 2 inches. A full cubic yard covers 108 square feet at 3 inches.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Depth</th><th class="num">Sq ft per cubic yard</th><th class="num">Sq ft per ton</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{coverage.map((c) => <tr><td>{c.d} in</td><td class="num">{fmt(c.yd, 0)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(c.ton, 0)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="depth">How deep should gravel be?</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>Typical depth</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Garden path or walkway</td><td>2–3 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pea gravel patio or play area</td><td>3 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decorative ground cover</td><td>2 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Driveway</td><td>4–6 in, usually a compacted base of larger stone topped with finer gravel</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Base under pavers or a shed</td><td>4–6 in of compacted crushed stone</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    For a layered driveway, run the calculator once per layer and add each to the project. The bedding layer under pavers
    is usually sand; the <a href="/sand-calculator/">sand calculator</a> handles that part.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bags-or-bulk">Bags or bulk delivery?</h2>
  <p>
    Bagged gravel usually comes in half-cubic-foot bags of about 50 lb, so a single cubic yard takes 54 bags. Bags make
    sense for topping up a path or filling a few planters. Past about half a yard, bulk delivery is almost always cheaper,
    even with a delivery fee.
  </p>

  <p>
    Building a paver patio? <a href="/paver-calculator/">Size the pavers, base, and sand in one step</a>. Mixing materials around the yard?
    <a href="/cubic-yard-calculator/">Convert each area to cubic yards</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How many cubic yards are in a ton of gravel?</h3>
  <p>About 0.71 cubic yards at 1.4 tons per yard. Divide tons by your stone's weight per yard to convert.</p>
  <h3>Should I order extra for compaction?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes for crusher run, road base, and anything you plan to tamp. Compacted base can lose 10–20% of its loose volume,
    so raise the waste setting to 15–20% for those layers.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I use this for rock, stone, or rubble?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. Choose the closest material or enter a custom weight per cubic yard from your supplier. For decorative stone
    such as river rock or lava rock, the <a href="/landscape-rock-calculator/">landscape rock calculator</a> has those
    types and depth guidance built in.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Volume = area × depth, converted to cubic yards (27 cubic feet per yard).</li>
    <li>Weight uses the typical density shown for each material, in US tons of 2,000 lb.</li>
    <li>Bulk orders round up to the next half yard or half ton; bag counts always round up.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
